  "query_preview": "..\n(Tonight through next Friday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: Satellite imagery for this morning is\nshowing a mix of relatively clear skies to the west and thin, high\nclouds in the east. This is all due to upper level high pressure\nthat is currently situated over the eastern Pacific. The \nresulting northerly flow will keep a lid on any active weather \nthrough at least the early to middle portions of next week. \nLooking at near surface conditions, temperature/dewpoint spreads \nduring the overnight have remained stubbornly close to each other,\nwith the spread about 5F or less. There is a low probability of \nfog/freezing fog formation in the valleys as a result. High \ntemperatures will slowly rise as heights slowly increase during \nthe upcoming weekend, with readings becoming above normal levels \nnext week. Overnight low temperatures will generally be in the 20s\nand 30s through the period. Afternoon breezes with gusts to 15 to\n20 mph are possible across the northernmost sections of the \nforecast area.\n\nBeginning next week Saturday, models are trying to hint at the \npossibility of a strong upper trough trying to push into Nevada. \nAlthough still very early, there are a few signs of an \natmospheric river component with this system that long range \nmodels are picking up on. So the possibility of a return of \nrain and snow showers will need to be watched over the several \ndays.\n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: \n\nThere is high confidence in upper ridge building over the Great \nBasin. This feature is expected to last through the mid- to late\nportions of next week, bringing dry conditions and relatively light\nwinds.\n\nModerate to high confidence exists with respect to an upper level\ntrough passage across the Pacific Northwest through this weekend.\nThis will bring slightly higher winds across far northern Nevada \nthis weekend.\n     \nThere is low to very low confidence in the upper pattern\ntransitioning to a strong trough at the end of next week as \nmodels continue to hint at a possible return to active weather \nacross the western United States.",
